          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 55-year-old smoker presents with ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis on the right s</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 55-year-old smoker presents with ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis on the right side. Where along the sympathetic pathway is the lesion most likely located?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>First neuron — hypothalamus to ciliospinal centre</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A first-neuron lesion (e.g., lateral medullary stroke) can produce anhidrosis, but smoking is not a risk factor for brainstem or spinal cord lesions at this level.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Second neuron — ciliospinal centre to superior cervical ganglion</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The second neuron loops over the lung apex. A Pancoast tumour in a smoker is the classic cause, and second-neuron lesions produce all three signs including anhidrosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Third neuron — superior cervical ganglion to orbit</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Third-neuron lesions (along the internal carotid) cause ptosis and miosis but spare anhidrosis, because sudomotor fibres travel with the external carotid artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Postganglionic parasympathetic fibres in the ciliary ganglion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The ciliary ganglion relays parasympathetic fibres for pupil constriction — it is not part of the sympathetic pathway and its damage would cause a dilated pupil, not miosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Key rule: anhidrosis appears only in first or second neuron lesions. In a smoker, the second neuron is vulnerable at the lung apex where a Pancoast tumour can compress it."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. The combination of a smoking history and anhidrosis localises the lesion to the second neuron at the lung apex — a Pancoast tumour must be excluded."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
