          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with sudden complete ptosis and the eye deviated down and out</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with sudden complete ptosis and the eye deviated down and out. Which single finding makes this a surgical emergency requiring immediate imaging?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Severe diplopia on attempted gaze</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Diplopia occurs in both surgical and medical CN III palsies and does not distinguish between them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A fixed, dilated pupil</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Parasympathetic fibres run on the surface of CN III and are compressed first by an expanding aneurysm (typically posterior communicating artery), causing pupil dilation. This demands emergency imaging.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Complete ptosis covering the visual axis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Complete ptosis indicates full levator denervation but occurs in both ischaemic and compressive lesions — it does not localise the cause.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inability to elevate or depress the eye</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Restricted vertical gaze reflects extraocular muscle denervation, which is common to all complete CN III palsies regardless of aetiology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: a dilated pupil distinguishes surgical (compressive) from medical (ischaemic) CN III palsy. Ischaemic palsy from diabetes or hypertension typically spares the pupil."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The pupil is the key — parasympathetic fibres lie superficially on CN III and are vulnerable to external compression, classically from a posterior communicating artery aneurysm."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
